## Pace yourself

Try not to do a lot on good days and then pay for it on bad days. Break tasks into smaller parts, take short breaks, and build up activity slowly.

## Move little and often

- change position every 30 minutes if you sit for long periods
- walk a little every day, and build up over time
- keep doing gentle stretches, even when the pain has eased

## At a desk or on your phone

- have your screen at eye level and your feet flat on the floor
- hold your phone higher so you are not looking down for long periods
- ask your employer for a **workstation assessment**

## Lifting safely

Keep the load close to your body, bend your knees, and avoid twisting. Ask for help with heavy or awkward items.

## Sleep

A comfortable position matters more than the type of mattress. A pillow between your knees (on your side) or under your knees (on your back) may help.

## Mood and stress

Long-term pain and low mood often go together, and each can make the other worse. Talk to your GP if you feel low or anxious. You can refer yourself to **NHS Talking Therapies** in England.

## Support

**Versus Arthritis** has a free helpline, and the **Live Well with Pain** website has free tools for managing long-term pain.
